maniT Balanced Ternary Demo
===========================

=================================================
  1. Trit Basics
=================================================
Trit literals:
  +  -> int value: 1
  0  -> int value: 0
  -  -> int value: -1

tif branching on trit values:
  tif + => positive branch
  tif 0 => zero branch
  tif - => negative branch

=================================================
  2. Ternary Word Types
=================================================
tryte examples (3 trits, range -13..+13):
  0t++- = 11 = 11
  0t--+ = -11 = -11
  0t+0- = 8 = 8

t9 examples (9 trits, range -9841..+9841):
  0t+00000000 = 6561
  0t+-------- = 3281

t27 examples (27 trits):
  0t+0-+0-+0- ... = +0-+0-+0-
  as integer     = 6056

=================================================
  3. Balanced Ternary Arithmetic
=================================================
x = 42
y = -17

x in balanced ternary (LST-first): 0---+
y in balanced ternary (LST-first): +0+-

t27 arithmetic (values as balanced ternary strings):
  a (42)  = +---0
  b (-17) = -+0+
  a << 1 (x3) = +---00 = 126
  a >> 1 (/3) = +--- = 14
  -a (tnot)   = -+++0 = -42

=================================================
  4. Ternary Operators (tand / tor / tnot / txor)
=================================================
Single-trit operations:
  tnot + = -
  tnot 0 = 0
  tnot - = +

tand (min) truth table:
       +    0    -
  +    +    0    -  
  0    +    0    -  
  -    -    -    -  

tor (max) truth table:
       +    0    -
  +    +    +    +  
  0    +    0    -  
  -    +    0    -  

txor (mod-3 add, balanced) truth table:
       +    0    -
  +    0    +    +  
  0    +    0    +  
  -    +    +    0  

Practical example: signal combining with tand / tor
  enable(+) tand fault(-) = -

=================================================
  5. Packing and Unpacking Trits
=================================================
Packed [+,-,0,+,+,-] (LST-first) = -++0-+  (int: -137)
Unpacked first 6 trits (LST-first): + - 0 + + - 
tryte_from_trits(+, 0, -) = 8  = 8

=================================================
  6. Ternary vs Binary Storage Efficiency
=================================================
Capacity comparison:
  Format      | Digits | Distinct values        | Bits equivalent
  ------------|--------|------------------------|----------------
  8-bit int   |   8    | 256                    | 8.0
  1-tryte     |   3    | 27                     | 4.75
  t9          |   9    | 19,683                 | 14.3
  16-bit int  |  16    | 65,536                 | 16.0
  t27         |  27    | 7,625,597,484,987      | 42.8
  32-bit int  |  32    | 4,294,967,296          | 32.0
  t54         |  54    | ~5.8e25                | 85.6
  64-bit int  |  64    | 1.8e19                 | 64.0

Information per digit:
  Binary trit (bit): log2(2) = 1.000 bit
  Balanced trit:     log2(3) = 1.585 bits  (+58.5% efficiency)

A t27 encodes as much information as a 42.8-bit binary integer,
using the same number of 'digits'.

Ternary logarithm examples (base 3):
  log3(1) = 0
  log3(3) = 1
  log3(9) = 2
  log3(27) = 3
  log3(81) = 4
  log3(243) = 5
  log3(729) = 6

=================================================
  7. Integer <-> Balanced Ternary Conversion
=================================================
n       | trit_count | balanced ternary (LST-first)
--------|------------|-----------------------------
       0  |     1      | 0
       1  |     1      | +
      -1  |     1      | -
       5  |     3      | --+
      -5  |     3      | ++-
      13  |     3      | +++
     -13  |     3      | ---
      42  |     5      | 0---+
     100  |     5      | +0-++
    -100  |     5      | -0+--
     255  |     6      | 0++00+
    1000  |     7      | +00+0++

Round-trip check (int -> ternary -> int):
  42 -> 0---+ -> 42  [OK]
  -99 -> 00+-- -> -99  [OK]
  1234 -> +0-+0--+ -> 1234  [OK]
  -5678 -> +0-0-++0- -> -5678  [OK]

Demo complete.
